<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1916 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Shchedryk</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="uk">Щедрик</bdi> / <bdi lang="uk-Latn">Shchedryk</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Ukrainian New Year song about a swallow that flies into a home to promise a good year. Its four-note tune is now known around the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional melody, arranged by Mykola Leontovych</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Ukrainians (New Year well-wishing song, shchedrivka)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Kyiv, Ukraine (arrangement); the folk song from Ukraine</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>folk chant of ancient origin; arranged by Leontovych between 1901 and 1919; first performed 29 December 1916</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics traditional; choral arrangement by Mykola Leontovych (first performed 1916))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shchedryk">Shchedryk</a>&quot; is a Ukrainian shchedrivka, a song sung to wish a household well at the New Year; its name comes from the Ukrainian word for &quot;bountiful&quot;, and in English it is known as &quot;The Little Swallow&quot;. The four-note chant at its heart, moving within a minor third, is thought to be of prehistoric origin, older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and belonged to a New Year then celebrated in March. With Christianity the New Year moved to January, and the song became tied to Shchedryi vechir, &quot;Bountiful Evening&quot;, the eve of the Julian New Year (13 January in the Gregorian calendar), known as Malanka. On that night such well-wishing songs were sung across Ukraine, often by girls who went from house to house and were given treats. The old words were thought to have magical power.</p><p>The conductor Alexander Koshetz asked the composer and teacher Mykola Leontovych to write a piece on Ukrainian folk melodies for a Christmas concert. Leontovych took the four notes and the folk words of a well-wishing song from an anthology of Ukrainian folk melodies and made a new work for unaccompanied mixed choir, with the four-note figure repeated throughout as an ostinato; he returned to the song in several arrangements between 1901 and 1919. It is often said to have been first sung by students of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> University on 25 December 1916, but its first performance was on 29 December 1916 in the Kyiv Merchants' Assembly Hall, now part of the National Philharmonic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It is the best known of all his works.</p><p>The newly independent Ukrainian government sent Koshetz's Ukrainian Republic Capella abroad in 1920 and 1921 to make Ukrainian music known. The choir gave more than 1,000 concerts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, sang &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shchedryk">Shchedryk</a>&quot; at Carnegie Hall on 5 October 1922; the first recording was made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> that month for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> Records. The American composer and teacher Peter J. Wilhousky of NBC Radio then wrote English words of his own about ringing bells, suggested by the ostinato and unrelated to the Ukrainian text, and published and copyrighted them in 1936 as &quot;Carol of the Bells&quot;. Sung by his choirs at Christmas from the late 1930s, the melody became closely associated with Christmas in the English-speaking world.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> the song kept its New Year meaning and is sung again on the eve of the Julian New Year rather than as a Christmas carol. It is heard in films from the lost &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Flame&quot; (1930) to the Ukrainian drama &quot;Carol of the Bells&quot; (2022), where children sing it as a sign of peace and hope. In 2023, because of the Russian invasion of Ukraine, the Eurovision Song Contest was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> rather than in Ukraine, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shchedryk">Shchedryk</a>&quot; was sung at the second semi-final by the Ukrainian singers Zlata Dziunka and Mariya Yaremchuk.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A well-wishing song for the turn of the year. A swallow flies into the household and announces that the family will prosper, with plenty to come in the spring. It is a song of good fortune and hope, not of Christmas.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Ukrainian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Ukrainian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Щедрик, щедрик, щедрівочка,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ščedryk, ščedryk, ščedrivočka,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shchedryk, shchedryk, little shchedrivka,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Прилетіла ластівочка!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Pryletila lastivočka!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A little swallow has flown in!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Стала собі щебетати,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Stala sobi ščebetaty,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She began to twitter,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Господаря викликати:</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Hospodarja vyklykaty:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To call the master out:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">- Вийди, вийди, господарю,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">- Vyjdy, vyjdy, hospodarju,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>- Come out, come out, master,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Подивися на кошару!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Podyvysja na košaru!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Look at the sheepfold!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Там овечки покотились</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Tam ovečky pokotylysʹ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There the ewes have lambed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">І ягнички народились.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">I jahnyčky narodylysʹ.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And little lambs are born.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">В тебе весь товар хороший,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">V tebe vesʹ tovar xorošyj,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All your stock is good,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Будеш мати мірку грошей.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Budeš maty mirku hrošej.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You will have a measure of money.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Хоч не гроші, то полова....</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Xoč ne hroši, to polova....</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If not money, then chaff....</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">В тебе жінка чорноброва.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">V tebe žinka čornobrova.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You have a black-browed wife.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Щедрик, щедрик, щедрівочка,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Ščedryk, ščedryk, ščedrivočka,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shchedryk, shchedryk, little shchedrivka,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="uk">Прилетіла ластівочка!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="uk-Latn">Pryletila lastivočka!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A little swallow has flown in!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ukrainian folk shchedrivka; the text of Mykola Leontovych's arrangement. Text from <a href="https://uk.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%A9%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%2C_%D1%89%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B8%D0%BA%2C_%D1%89%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%80%D1%96%D0%B2%D0%BE%D1%87%D0%BA%D0%B0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>lines 1.1, 1.13: 'щедрик' and 'щедрівочка' are kept as names: from 'щедрий' (generous), the carol (shchedrivka) sung on the Generous Evening (Shchedryi vechir, the eve of the Old New Year); '-очка' is a diminutive, so 'little shchedrivka'.</li><li>line 1.11: 'Even if not money, then chaff' is the sense disputed most: either a wish of plenty however it comes (chaff, the husks of threshed grain, as a lesser wealth) or a teasing turn before the compliment of 1.12. Rendered literally; the '....' is the source's.</li><li>line 1.7: 'покотились' is the folk verb for sheep giving birth (to lamb), not 'rolled'.</li><li>line 1.9: 'товар' here is livestock, the household's goods on the hoof, not merchandise.</li><li>line 1.10: 'мірка', a measure (a dry measure, as of grain): money by the measure.</li><li>line 1.12: 'чорноброва', black-browed, the folk praise of a beautiful woman.</li><li>line 1.4: 'господар' is the master of the household (farmer), not a lord.</li><li>line 1.5: the '- ' dash marks the swallow's words; kept as in the source.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="XWsgqwk0EGo" data-title="Shchedryk: Veryovka Ukrainian Folk Choir, Leontovych's arrangement"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Veryovka Ukrainian Folk Choir, Leontovych's arrangement</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel VeryovkaMusic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XWsgqwk0EGo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Carol of the Bells (Peter J. Wilhousky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new English words (in copyright)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ring, Christmas Bells</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1947</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new devotional words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English adaptation of the Ukrainian words (Stepan Pasicznyk)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Flame (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1930</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in (sung by a choir at a harvest festival)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Carol of the Bells (Ukrainian film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hunt for the Wilderpeople (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Immaculate (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2024</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Deep Throats (Family Guy episode)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shchedryk (Vopli Vidopliassova)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2012</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka (the well-wishing song in a folk anthology used by Leontovych)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody and words</span></li></ul></section>
